<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  preflight.launch — the readiness check. Run this before every session.

  It starts nothing and changes nothing. It inspects the running system and
  tells you whether the data you are about to record will be worth keeping.

  Run it AFTER bringup.launch (and after the camera, if you are using one),
  because it checks a live system rather than a static configuration.

  USAGE
    roslaunch sidewalk_bringup preflight.launch
    roslaunch sidewalk_bringup preflight.launch strict:=true   # warnings block too

  Use strict:=true before a long recording session or any run whose results
  will go into the report. Use the default while developing, when a warning is
  usually something you already know about.

  WHERE THE FRAME LIST COMES FROM — it is NOT written in this file any more.
  It is loaded straight out of config/robot_frames.yaml, which is the file that
  owns the robot's geometry. See the first tag inside the node below for why
  that changed on 2026-08-29.
-->
<launch>

  <arg name="strict" default="false"
       doc="Treat warnings as blocking. Use before recording data that matters."/>
  <arg name="camera_expected" default="true"
       doc="Set false when deliberately running without the camera."/>

  <node name="preflight_check" pkg="sidewalk_bringup" type="preflight_check.py"
        output="screen" required="false">

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         THE FRAME LIST, LOADED FROM THE ONE FILE THAT OWNS IT.

         This tag REPLACES a hand-written second copy of expected_frames that
         used to live here, and removing that copy is the entire point of it.

         WHAT WENT WRONG, so nobody reintroduces it. The list existed in two
         places — here, and at the bottom of config/robot_frames.yaml — with
         nothing keeping them in step. On 2026-08-29 the copy in
         robot_frames.yaml was corrected to name the frame the ZED driver
         really publishes. This copy was not. And because preflight_check.py
         reads its PRIVATE parameter ~expected_frames, which only this file
         set, the correction changed nothing about the running check:
         bringup.launch loads robot_frames.yaml under /sidewalk/, so the
         corrected list sat at /sidewalk/expected_frames where the node never
         looked. The gate went on testing the stale list.

         HOW THIS TAG FIXES IT. A <rosparam command="load" file="..."/> placed
         inside a <node> loads into that node's PRIVATE namespace. So
         robot_frames.yaml's top-level `expected_frames:` key arrives as
         ~expected_frames — precisely the parameter the script already reads.
         There is now one list, in the file whose whole subject is the robot's
         geometry, and no way for a copy to drift from it.
         [VERIFIED 2026-08-29 on the Jetson with roslaunch's dump-params option
          (two leading hyphens, which cannot be written inside an XML comment)
          on a scratch launch file of exactly this shape: the file's
          expected_frames key came out as /preflight_check/expected_frames, and
          a <param> tag placed after this one still took effect.]

         THE WHOLE FILE IS LOADED, not just that one key, because roslaunch's
         rosparam tag cannot select a sub-key out of a file. robot_frames.yaml's
         other keys (base:, camera_front:, imu: ...) therefore land in this
         node's private namespace as well. That is harmless — the script reads
         three parameters and ignores everything else — and it is also mildly
         useful, since the geometry the check is implicitly about is then
         visible on the parameter server beside the check that ran.

         KEEP THIS TAG FIRST. roslaunch applies a node's parameter tags in
         document order, last write wins. First means (a) anything below can
         deliberately override the list, and (b) a key ever added to
         robot_frames.yaml that happened to share a name with one set below —
         `strict`, say — cannot silently clobber it. Last would invert both.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <rosparam command="load"
              file="$(find sidewalk_bringup)/config/robot_frames.yaml"/>

    <param name="strict" value="$(arg strict)"/>

    <!-- Tells the script whether an empty topic list is a deliberate choice or
         a misconfiguration. Without this the script cannot tell the two apart,
         and its only safe move is to stay quiet — which is how a check ends up
         doing nothing while printing nothing. -->
    <param name="camera_expected" value="$(arg camera_expected)"/>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Topics that must exist, with the rate each should hold.

         The check MEASURES the real rate rather than trusting the configured
         one. That gap is exactly where bandwidth and power problems hide: the
         Quad card shares bandwidth between camera groups, so a second camera
         on the same deserializer group can quietly halve the frame rate of
         the first. Nothing errors. The number just drops.

         THESE ARE PUBLISH RATES, AND A PUBLISH RATE IS NOT A GRAB RATE.
         sidewalk_perception/config/zedx_front.yaml (this repository, and its
         copy on the Jetson) sets two different numbers, and they differ on
         purpose: general/grab_frame_rate: 30 at line 58, how often the
         camera is read, and general/pub_frame_rate: 15 at line 59, how often
         the wrapper puts a frame on a topic. This check subscribes to
         topics, so the publish rate is the only one it can ever observe.

         BOTH IMAGE ENTRIES SAID 30.0 UNTIL 2026-08-29. With FPS_TOLERANCE =
         0.85 in preflight_check.py that set a 25.5 Hz floor under a stream
         configured to deliver 15, so a correctly configured camera warned on
         every single run — and under strict:=true, blocked the run.

         THE IMU ENTRY SAID 400.0, which is the ZED 2's figure, carried over
         to a camera whose datasheet states 200 Hz in two separate places (the
         "Motion Sensors" tables). The identical mistake was corrected the
         same day in perception_common.ZEDX["imu_rate_hz"] and in
         sidewalk_perception/config/validation_thresholds.yaml. 400 x 0.85 =
         340 Hz is a floor no healthy ZED X can reach, so that gate fired on
         every run too.

         THE CHECK IS ONE-SIDED — see check_topics() in preflight_check.py. It
         warns when the measured rate is BELOW want_hz x 0.85 and says nothing
         at all when it is above. So a number set too LOW here makes the gate
         silent rather than noisy, which is why these have to track
         zedx_front.yaml rather than being left comfortably generous.

         WHY THIS IS A LIST OF PAIRS AND NOT A PLAIN topic: rate MAPPING.
         It used to be a mapping, and that made this check DEAD — it never ran
         once, on any run, and said nothing while not running.

         The reason is a rosparam rule that is easy to walk into: when a
         <rosparam> value is a dictionary, roslaunch sets each of its keys as
         its own parameter, and a key BEGINNING WITH "/" is a GLOBAL name. Every
         key here is a topic, so every key begins with "/". The three entries
         therefore did not land under ~expected_topics at all — they were
         written to the global parameter server as /zedx_front/zed_node/... ,
         inside the live camera node's own parameter namespace, leaving
         ~expected_topics with no children whatsoever. The script's
         `rospy.get_param("~expected_topics", {})` fell through to its empty
         default and printed "No expected topics configured — skipping".
         [VERIFIED 2026-08-29 on the Jetson with roslaunch's dump-params option:
          on the old block it emitted /zedx_front/zed_node/left/image_rect_color
          = 30.0 as a GLOBAL parameter and no /preflight_check/expected_topics
          at all, while a test key without a leading slash landed correctly
          under /preflight_check/expected_topics/. The same command on the list
          form below returns the list whole and intact.]

         A YAML LIST is opaque to that key-splitting — it is stored as one
         value — so writing the pairs as a list keeps them where the script can
         read them. Do not "tidy" this back into a mapping.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <rosparam if="$(arg camera_expected)" param="expected_topics">
      # 15.0 = general/pub_frame_rate in zedx_front.yaml, NOT the 30 of
      # general/grab_frame_rate. Both entries read 30.0 before 2026-08-29.
      - {topic: /zedx_front/zed_node/left/image_rect_color,  hz: 15.0}
      # Depth: same 15.0. [INFERENCE] pub_frame_rate is documented as one
      # publish rate covering video and depth together, and this project has
      # no copy of the zed-ros-wrapper source where this was written.
      # Settle it with the camera on, in one command:
      #     rostopic hz /zedx_front/zed_node/depth/depth_registered
      # If depth turns out to publish at the 30 Hz grab rate, raise this
      # entry to 30.0 — leaving it at 15.0 costs no false warning (the check
      # is one-sided) but does make the depth gate blind to a halving.
      - {topic: /zedx_front/zed_node/depth/depth_registered, hz: 15.0}
      # 200.0 = the ZED X datasheet's IMU output data rate, the same value
      # held in perception_common.ZEDX["imu_rate_hz"]. It read 400.0 before
      # 2026-08-29; 400 Hz is the ZED 2's figure and was carried over by
      # mistake. sensors/max_pub_rate in zedx_front.yaml is 400.0, but that
      # is a CEILING above the source rate and therefore a no-op — it is not
      # a second opinion about what this topic delivers.
      - {topic: /zedx_front/zed_node/imu/data,               hz: 200.0}
    </rosparam>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Frames that must be connected in the transform tree.

         A missing transform is the most dangerous failure in this list,
         because it does not stop anything. Programs that need the frame
         simply fall back to defaults or drop data, and you end up with a map
         built in the wrong reference — plausible-looking and wrong.

         THE LIST IS NOT HERE. It is loaded from config/robot_frames.yaml by
         the first tag in this node — read that tag before adding one back.
         The first entry in it is the reference; every other is checked
         against it.

         To check a DIFFERENT set of frames for a one-off, add a
         <rosparam param="expected_frames"> block BELOW this comment. Being
         later in the file, it overrides the loaded list. Do not make that
         permanent: a permanent second copy is the defect that was just fixed.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  </node>

</launch>
